        "explanation": "This is a TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX - an immediately life-threatening emergency requiring urgent decompression WITHOUT waiting for imaging. Delay for investigations can be fatal.\n\n**Clinical Features of Tension Pneumothorax:**\n\n**Respiratory Compromise:**\n- Severe dyspnoea, respiratory distress (RR 35/min)\n- Profound hypoxia (SpO2 78% despite high-flow oxygen)\n- Absent breath sounds on affected side (right)\n- Hyperresonance to percussion (right)\n\n**Cardiovascular Compromise (Obstructive Shock):**\n- Hypotension (BP 85/50 mmHg) - from reduced venous return\n- Tachycardia (HR 135 bpm) - compensatory\n- Distended neck veins (elevated JVP) - impaired venous drainage\n\n**Mediastinal Shift:**\n- Tracheal deviation AWAY from affected side (to left)\n- Compressed IVC → reduced preload → shock\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\nOne-way valve effect → air enters pleural space during inspiration but cannot escape → progressive accumulation → mediastinal shift → compressed IVC and contralateral lung → cardiovascular collapse.\n\n**Australian Emergency Management (eTG Complete, ATLS Guidelines):**\n\n**B. Needle Decompression 2nd ICS MCL - CORRECT:**\n\n**Why Immediate Decompression:**\n- Clinical diagnosis - do NOT wait for CXR\n- Every minute delay worsens hypoxia and shock\n- Decompression converts tension to simple pneumothorax, buys time for definitive chest drain\n\n**Technique (Australian ATLS Protocol):**\n1. **Location**: 2nd intercostal space (ICS), mid-clavicular line (MCL), AFFECTED SIDE (right in this case)\n2. **Equipment**: Large-bore cannula (14-16G), at least 4.5 cm long\n3. **Insertion**: Perpendicular to chest wall, just above 3rd rib (avoid neurovascular bundle below rib)\n4. **Confirmation**: Hiss of air escape under pressure\n5. **Leave cannula in situ**: Remove needle, leave plastic sheath, tape in place\n6. **Immediate improvement**: BP, HR, SpO2 should improve within 30-60 seconds\n7. **Definitive management**: Insert chest drain (5th ICS, mid-axillary line, triangle of safety) as soon as possible after stabilisation\n\n**Why Other Options are Incorrect:**\n\nA. **Chest X-ray first** - DANGEROUS DELAY. Tension pneumothorax is a CLINICAL diagnosis:\n- Respiratory distress + absent breath sounds + hyperresonance + haemodynamic compromise + tracheal deviation\n- CXR confirmation NOT required before intervention\n- Patient may arrest during transport to radiology\n- Perform CXR AFTER decompression to confirm and guide chest drain insertion\n\nC. **Chest drain under ultrasound** - definitive treatment but TOO SLOW for immediate emergency. Ultrasound-guided drain insertion takes 5-10 minutes (consent, positioning, sterile prep, local anaesthetic). This patient is peri-arrest - needs decompression in <60 seconds. Needle decompression first → then chest drain.\n\nD. **Intubation and positive pressure ventilation** - will WORSEN tension pneumothorax by forcing more air into pleural space. NEVER intubate a tension pneumothorax until AFTER decompression. If intubation necessary (severe hypoxia, airway protection), must decompress first.\n\nE. **Bronchodilators and steroids** - inappropriate. This is not an asthma/COPD exacerbation (though patient has COPD). Tension pneumothorax requires mechanical intervention, not pharmacotherapy.\n\n**Australian Context - Pitfalls:**\n\n**Chest Wall Thickness:**\n- Standard 4.5 cm cannula may be too short in obese patients (chest wall >5 cm)\n- If no air escape, consider anterior chest wall too thick\n- Alternative site: 5th ICS, mid-axillary line (thinner chest wall)\n\n**Follow-up After Needle Decompression:**\n- Always insert definitive chest drain (large-bore, 28-32 Fr)\n- Admit to ICU/HDU\n- CXR post-drain insertion\n- Watch for re-tension if drain blocked/kinked\n\n**Secondary Spontaneous Pneumothorax (SSP) Risk:**\n- This patient has COPD (underlying lung disease) = SSP\n- Higher risk of persistent air leak, recurrence\n- May require surgical intervention (VATS pleurodesis) if leak >5 days",
