        "explanation": "This question tests application of Light's criteria - the gold standard for differentiating exudative from transudative pleural effusions.\n\n**Light's Criteria (Published 1972, Still Gold Standard):**\n\nA pleural effusion is an EXUDATE if ONE or MORE of the following criteria are met:\n\n1. **Pleural fluid protein / Serum protein >0.5**\n2. **Pleural fluid LDH / Serum LDH >0.6**\n3. **Pleural fluid LDH >2/3 upper limit of normal serum LDH** (approximately >200 U/L)\n\n**Applying Light's Criteria to This Patient:**\n\n**Criterion 1: Protein Ratio**\n- Pleural fluid protein: 55 g/L\n- Serum protein: 70 g/L\n- Ratio: 55/70 = **0.79** (>0.5) ✓ **EXUDATE**\n\n**Criterion 2: LDH Ratio**\n- Pleural fluid LDH: 580 U/L\n- Serum LDH: 200 U/L\n- Ratio: 580/200 = **2.9** (>0.6) ✓ **EXUDATE**\n\n**Criterion 3: Absolute Pleural LDH**\n- Pleural fluid LDH: 580 U/L (>200 U/L) ✓ **EXUDATE**\n\n**Result: Meets ALL THREE criteria → EXUDATE**\n\n**B. Exudate, Further Investigation Required - CORRECT:**\n\nOnce classified as exudate, must investigate underlying cause:\n\n**Common Causes of Exudative Effusions (Australian Context):**\n\n**1. Infection (40%):**\n- Parapneumonic effusion (adjacent pneumonia)\n- Empyema (purulent)\n- Tuberculosis (lymphocyte-predominant)\n\n**2. Malignancy (30%):**\n- Lung cancer (adenocarcinoma most common)\n- Mesothelioma (asbestos exposure history)\n- Metastatic cancer (breast, ovarian)\n- Lymphoma\n\n**3. Pulmonary Embolism (10%):**\n- Often small, blood-stained\n- May have pleuritic chest pain\n\n**4. Autoimmune (10%):**\n- Rheumatoid arthritis (low glucose <1.6 mmol/L, low pH)\n- SLE\n- Wegener's granulomatosis\n\n**5. Other (10%):**\n- Post-cardiac injury syndrome\n- Pancreatitis (elevated amylase)\n- Oesophageal rupture\n\n**Further Investigations Required (eTG Complete 2024):**\n\n**Pleural Fluid Analysis:**\n- **Cytology**: Malignant cells (sensitivity 60% for cancer)\n- **Microbiology**: Gram stain, culture (bacterial, TB, fungal)\n- **Cell count and differential**: Neutrophils (infection), lymphocytes (TB, malignancy), eosinophils (drugs, PE)\n- **Glucose**: <1.6 mmol/L suggests empyema, rheumatoid arthritis, malignancy\n- **pH**: <7.2 suggests complicated parapneumonic effusion/empyema (needs drain)\n- **Adenosine deaminase (ADA)**: >40 U/L suggests TB (high sensitivity/specificity in high-prevalence areas)\n\n**This Patient's Fluid:**\n- pH 7.42 (normal - excludes empyema)\n- Glucose 4.5 mmol/L (normal - excludes empyema/rheumatoid)\n- Need cytology, culture, cell count differential\n\n**Imaging:**\n- **CT chest with contrast**: Identify underlying lung lesion, lymphadenopathy, pleural thickening\n- **Ultrasound thorax**: Guide repeat thoracentesis if loculated\n\n**Biopsy (if above investigations non-diagnostic):**\n- **CT-guided pleural biopsy**: Sensitivity 87% for malignancy, 79% for TB\n- **Thoracoscopy (VATS) with biopsy**: Gold standard if CT-biopsy negative (sensitivity >95%)\n\n**Why Other Options are Incorrect:**\n\nA. **Transudate** - wrong classification. Meets Light's criteria for exudate.\n\n**Transudate Causes (NOT this patient):**\n- Cardiac failure (most common)\n- Cirrhosis (hepatic hydrothorax)\n- Nephrotic syndrome\n- Hypoalbuminaemia\n\nTransudates have LOW protein (<30 g/L), LOW LDH (<200 U/L), do NOT meet Light's criteria.\n\nC. **Borderline** - no, clearly meets criteria (all 3). Borderline would be ratios near cutoffs (e.g., protein ratio 0.48-0.52).\n\nD. **Haemothorax** - would have frank blood, elevated pleural fluid haematocrit (>50% of serum haematocrit). No mention of blood-stained fluid here.\n\nE. **Chylothorax** - milky/turbid fluid, elevated triglycerides (>1.24 mmol/L). Causes: lymphoma, thoracic duct injury. Not suggested by this presentation.\n\n**Australian Management Pathway:**\n\n**All Exudates:**\n→ CT chest\n→ Pleural fluid cytology, culture, ADA\n→ If non-diagnostic → pleural biopsy (CT-guided or thoracoscopy)\n\n**If Malignant:**\n→ Oncology referral\n→ Consider pleurodesis (talc) if symptomatic recurrent effusion\n\n**If TB:**\n→ Respiratory/infectious diseases referral\n→ 6-month anti-TB therapy (RIPE regimen)\n\n**If Empyema (pH <7.2, glucose <2.2):**\n→ Chest drain + IV antibiotics\n→ Consider intrapleural fibrinolytics if loculated\n→ Cardiothoracic surgery if fails medical management",
