        "explanation": "This patient has a classic presentation of hepatic hydrothorax - a transudative pleural effusion complicating advanced cirrhosis.\n\n**Light's Criteria Applied:**\n\n**Criterion 1: Pleural Protein / Serum Protein**\n- 18 / 52 = **0.35** (<0.5) → TRANSUDATE ✓\n\n**Criterion 2: Pleural LDH / Serum LDH**\n- 85 / 180 = **0.47** (<0.6) → TRANSUDATE ✓\n\n**Criterion 3: Absolute Pleural LDH**\n- 85 U/L (<200 U/L) → TRANSUDATE ✓\n\n**Result: Meets ZERO criteria for exudate → TRANSUDATE**\n\n**B. Hepatic Hydrothorax (Transudate) - CORRECT:**\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\n1. **Portal hypertension** → ascites formation (third-spacing of fluid)\n2. **Diaphragmatic defects** (congenital or acquired) at tendinous portion (usually right hemidiaphragm)\n3. **Negative intrathoracic pressure during inspiration** sucks ascitic fluid through defects into pleural space\n4. **One-way valve effect** → fluid accumulates in pleural space\n5. **Right-sided predominance** (70%) - defects more common on right diaphragm\n\n**Clinical Features:**\n- Advanced cirrhosis (portal hypertension)\n- Dyspnoea (often out of proportion to ascites)\n- Right-sided effusion >>left (70% vs 15%, 15% bilateral)\n- Ascites may be minimal or absent (fluid preferentially in pleural space)\n- Clear transudate on thoracentesis\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- **Transudate by Light's criteria** (as in this case)\n- **Peritoneal-pleural gradient**: Protein in pleural fluid HIGHER than in ascites (if ascites present to sample)\n- **Radioisotope study**: Tc-99m labelled albumin in peritoneum → detected in pleural space (confirms diaphragmatic communication)\n- **CT/MRI**: May visualize diaphragmatic defects (1-3 cm)\n\n**Australian Management (eTG Complete 2024):**\n\n**1. Treat Underlying Cirrhosis:**\n- **Salt restriction**: <2 g sodium/day\n- **Diuretics**: Spironolactone 100 mg + frusemide 40 mg daily (maintain K+ 4-5 mmol/L)\n- **Fluid restriction**: If hyponatraemia (Na <125 mmol/L)\n\n**2. Therapeutic Thor