otein >40 g/L\n- LDH >200 U/L\n- Glucose often low (<2.2 mmol/L)\n- ADA >40 U/L (highly sensitive/specific for TB)\n- Clear but can be straw-coloured\nThis transudate excludes TB.\n\nE. **Parapneumonic effusion** - EXUDATE:\n- Neutrophil-predominant\n- Protein >30 g/L\n- LDH >200 U/L\n- If complicated: pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L\n- Adjacent pneumonia on CXR/CT\nThis transudate excludes bacterial pneumonia.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Transudative Effusions:**\n\n**1. Cardiac Failure (most common 40%):**\n- Bilateral, cardiomegaly on CXR\n- Elevated BNP/NT-proBNP\n- Echocardiography: reduced EF or diastolic dysfunction\n\n**2. Hepatic Hydrothorax (20%):**\n- Cirrhosis, right-sided\n- Minimal or absent ascites\n- Normal cardiac function\n\n**3. Nephrotic Syndrome (10%):**\n- Heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/24 hours)\n- Hypoalbuminaemia (<25 g/L)\n- Peripheral oedema, anasarca\n\n**4. Hypoalbuminaemia (<20 g/L) (5%):**\n- Malnutrition\n- Protein-losing enteropathy\n- Severe liver disease\n\n**5. Peritoneal Dialysis (rare):**\n- Dialysate crosses diaphragm\n- Glucose in pleural fluid >5 mmol/L\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- TIPS available at major tertiary centres (Royal Prince Alfred, St Vincent's Sydney, Austin Melbourne, Royal Brisbane)\n- Refer to hepatology early if refractory\n- Hepatic hydrothorax is Model for End-stage Liver Disease (MELD) exception points for transplant listing",
