        "explanation": "This patient has developed re-expansion pulmonary oedema (REPO) - a serious complication of rapid drainage of large pleural effusions.\n\n**Re-Expansion Pulmonary Oedema (REPO):**\n\n**B. Re-Expansion Pulmonary Oedema - CORRECT:**\n\n**Definition:**\nPulmonary oedema (unilateral or bilateral) occurring within 24 hours of rapid re-expansion of collapsed lung following drainage of pneumothorax or pleural effusion.\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\n\n1. **Chronic lung collapse** (from large effusion) → alveolar capillary damage\n2. **Rapid re-expansion** → sudden increase in capillary hydrostatic pressure\n3. **Increased capillary permeability** → fluid leaks into alveolar space\n4. **Inflammatory response** → surfactant dysfunction, oxidative injury\n5. **Pulmonary oedema** → bilateral (50%) or ipsilateral (50%)\n\n**Risk Factors:**\n- **Large volume drainage**: >1.5 L\n- **Rapid drainage**: <10-15 minutes (this patient: 2 L in 10 minutes)\n- **Young age**: <40 years (but can occur at any age)\n- **Chronic effusion/pneumothorax**: >72 hours duration (lung chronically collapsed)\n- **Negative pleural pressure**: >-20 cmH2O during drainage (lung 'sucked' open forcefully)\n\n**Clinical Features (This Patient):**\n- **Timing**: Immediate or within 1-2 hours of drainage (90% within 24 hours)\n- **Symptoms**: Chest tightness, cough (productive - frothy pink sputum if severe), dyspnoea\n- **Signs**: Tachypnoea (RR 30), hypoxia (SpO2 88%), bibasal crackles (pulmonary oedema)\n- **CXR**: Bilateral alveolar infiltrates (bat-wing pattern) or ipsilateral consolidation\n\n**Australian Management (eTG Complete 2024):**\n\n**1. Immediate:**\n- **Stop drainage immediately** (already done - procedure complete)\n- **High-flow oxygen**: 15 L/min via Hudson mask or non-rebreather, target SpO2 >92%\n- **Sit upright**: Reduces venous return, improves oxygenation\n- **Nil by mouth**: Risk of aspiration if worsening\n\n**2. Supportive Care:**\n- **Non-invasive ventilation (NIV